\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The first response of the connected fourth moment}
\date{2026-11-09}

\p{Use orthonormal coordinates on a finite real Hilbert space,
#{Q>0}, and
##{V(\phi)=\frac1{4!}\sum_v g_v\phi_v^4,\qquad g_v>0.}
For any nonzero coordinate vector #{f}, let #{Y=f\cdot\phi}
and let #{\kappa_{4,\lambda}(Y)} be its
\vocabk{joint cumulant}{fdip-00GM} with 4 equal arguments under
#{\mu_\lambda}. Then
##{\left.\frac{d}{d\lambda}\kappa_{4,\lambda}(Y)\right|_{0+}
 =-\sum_v g_v\bigl((Q^{-1}f)_v\bigr)^4<0.}
Consequently, for each fixed nonzero #{f}, there is #{\epsilon_f>0}
such that the actual law of #{Y} is non-Gaussian whenever
#{0<\lambda<\epsilon_f}. If #{H\ne0}, the full law
#{\mu_\lambda} is therefore non-Gaussian for sufficiently small
positive coupling.}

\proof{
\p{Put #{\Sigma=Q^{-1}} and #{a=\Sigma f}. Under the Gaussian
source tilt #{e^{tY}}, square completion changes the mean to #{ta}
and leaves covariance #{\Sigma} fixed. The scalar Gaussian fourth
moment therefore gives
##{\mathbb E_{0,tf}\phi_v^4
 =t^4a_v^4+6t^2a_v^2\Sigma_{vv}+3\Sigma_{vv}^2.}
Here #{tf} denotes the source #{\phi\mapsto tf\cdot\phi}.
By \ref{fdip-00GO}, the coupling response of the normalized
source logarithm is
##{\begin{aligned}
\left.\partial_\lambda W_\lambda(tf)\right|_{0+}
 &=-\mathbb E_{0,tf}V+\mathbb E_{\mu_Q}V\\
 &=-\frac1{24}\sum_vg_v
      (t^4a_v^4+6t^2a_v^2\Sigma_{vv}).
\end{aligned}}
The constant terms cancel because #{W_\lambda(0)=0}.
Taking 4 derivatives in #{t} and commuting them with the right
coupling derivative proves the formula. Invertibility of #{\Sigma}
gives #{a\ne0} whenever #{f\ne0}; at least one positive summand
then makes its sign strict.}

\p{At #{\lambda=0}, the fourth cumulant is zero. Continuity of
the second coupling derivative, again from \ref{fdip-00GO},
gives the actual expansion
##{\kappa_{4,\lambda}(Y)
 =-\lambda\sum_vg_va_v^4+O_f(\lambda^2)
 \qquad(\lambda\downarrow0).}
It is strictly negative on a sufficiently short positive interval.
A real Gaussian variable has a quadratic source logarithm and zero
fourth cumulant, proving the assertion. A Gaussian full law would
have Gaussian linear images, so any one such #{f} proves the final
claim. For #{H=0} there is no nonzero #{f} and no non-Gaussianity
assertion.}
}

\p{The centered fourth-moment identity is
\citet{v2, Theorem 3.1 and Example 3.2, p. 29}{etingof2024mathematical};
its use after a linear source shift and the coupling response are
the deductions above.}
